hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"21581","Abdullah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahim Abu Yahya al-Bazzaz narrated to us, Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Mu'adh ibn Muhammad ibn Mu'adh ibn Ubayy ibn Ka'b narrated to us, my father Muhammad ibn Mu'adh narrated to me, from Mu'adh, from Muhammad, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that Abu Hurayrah used to be bold in asking the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, about things others did not ask him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the first you saw in the matter of prophethood? The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, sat up and said: You have asked, Abu Hurayrah. I was in a desert, a boy of ten years and months, and there was speech above my head, and a man saying to a man: Is it he? He said: Yes. They faced me with faces I never saw in creation, and souls I never smelled in creation, and clothes I never saw on anyone. They came to me walking until each one took my upper arm, I felt no touch of either. One said to his companion: Lay him down. So they laid me down without force or violence. One said to his companion: Split his chest. So one of them inclined to my chest and split it, as I saw, without blood or pain. He said to him: Take out rancor and envy. So he took out something like a clot, then cast it away. He said to him: Enter compassion and mercy. And it was like what he took out, resembling silver. Then he shook my right big toe and said: Go and be safe. So I returned with it, hastening, tender to the small and merciful to the great."
